# Local Android SDK path, written by the Flutter tool.
/android/local.properties

# Recitation masters. They live on the `audio-masters` GitHub release, not in
# git: see audio/README.md. Only the converted, shipping audio is committed.
/audio/source/

# Generated by tools/timing_tool/build.py from the timing file and content.db.
tools/timing_tool/*.html
!tools/timing_tool/page.html

# The signing keystore, anywhere in the tree.
#
# `android/.gitignore` also lists these, but a .gitignore only governs its own
# directory downwards — and `keytool` writes the keystore into whatever
# directory it was run from, which for anybody following docs/signing.md is
# the repository root. So the file most likely to be created is the one
# android/.gitignore cannot see. Checked by running `git check-ignore` against
# a root-level .jks and finding it was not ignored.
#
# Losing this file means the app can never be updated under the same identity
# again; leaking it means anybody can sign a package Android, Play and
# assetlinks.json all accept as Hazri.
*.jks
*.keystore
key.properties

# Phase 2 secrets. These are the ones neither of the above covers. The first commit after a
# Firebase project or an email sender is created is where they leak, and an
# API key in history is a spam relay somebody else owns.
#
# Safe to commit: a certificate's SHA-256 fingerprint, and google-services.json
# while this repository stays private — inject it at build time if it ever
# goes public.
**/service-account*.json
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
/functions/.runtimeconfig.json
/functions/.env*
